#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167904} :activity/id #uuid "70c56636-a16b-5974-b39b-c312a1a1e556" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049209 :topic/id #uuid "e362b30c-c362-5928-a478-dbcc78543cc7" :topic/math-academy-id 3241} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167904 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168009 :tutorial/content "In this topic, we'll study phase portraits of linear systems\n![](images/b0/b083c5707c4e9aed3141fe638c55a1ed2dc3ad6569f122bef307550c94fe6a8b.svg)\nwhere the eigenvalues of a ![](images/25/2521651122403a440e682fef383af333958f5b0d1334abe8b0771865d4daf100.svg) matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) are\n![](images/ee/eee119e3e35f381a1d66d9e1e75b27d89e68d5f72307f4e792887d5d7e9d0099.svg)\n\nLet ![](images/37/37030865839993f96fced21145844e8fca9c6d44e7922c623fe8e0bef98ef154.svg) where ![](images/0d/0d26960845a9f13b07c87e80faa88162c1dda64123f13567958d00aaa0a4dc94.svg) be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue ![](images/fa/faa0f885195eacb91222dc9ccc18cf7ab891a17bd787359d13bf4e72bf09d703.svg)\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n![](images/c4/c44ac9f2de14beffd3411373047316c888a93815d2f90d338f500ca329f66355.svg)\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n![](images/e9/e90f20affdff00fd5fb24e5b6f75c31d42a0e5b2ad52b38b2d4456ee6555bfc8.svg)\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n![](images/62/6224eb38da8f189696cbda057a5568d71310d64574d36e80af6e1a11ceed6d6e.svg)\n\nNotice that ![](images/42/42bbe4d12055825e9c24b2dfc1a6ca306cb2675c13d039fbdb67e2170788c612.svg) and ![](images/ca/ca0400387ee619b6e2ddc7028859cf5902aecb715c38ec71e43b0f9056533cb6.svg) are bounded, so the factor ![](images/b0/b051bd1eb312485960edb54d28ffe85da63acab1c5f20b59466d01f55cba034c.svg) controls the long-term behavior:\n\n- If ![](images/0a/0a23a6577131711d405eb650b1d9d8429ca07d07bb9759644c407121643dcf5e.svg) then ![](images/9a/9a15a0d668c2a7ab9457f6a4c605858e03ddbb3ff93f3667d0deb0def1e206b1.svg) as ![](images/e5/e54727d514ed264e6dd30c7f4044746e6d2b4b462dc3776daecf7eb68634c358.svg) So, trajectories are *unbounded*, and we have a *spiral* that is traversed toward infinity as ![](images/e5/e54727d514ed264e6dd30c7f4044746e6d2b4b462dc3776daecf7eb68634c358.svg) In particular, the trajectory is unbounded for ![](images/bb/bb0d93faa0ccc44575fe83f9f7a644895ced86c18e9908a8e0d6dc6a36378d7d.svg)\n\n- If ![](images/28/28e6012b2b10c0a7f40681ab8a73666a12afd6e976c59f536ed30d1defca5f64.svg) then ![](images/2b/2b74153553b7dab9ad3a92e31ddd3b09ba44e4ba0e451229e924f227087fb58b.svg) as ![](images/e5/e54727d514ed264e6dd30c7f4044746e6d2b4b462dc3776daecf7eb68634c358.svg) So, trajectories *approach the origin*, and we have a *spiral* that is traversed toward the origin as ![](images/e5/e54727d514ed264e6dd30c7f4044746e6d2b4b462dc3776daecf7eb68634c358.svg) In particular, the trajectory is bounded for ![](images/23/23f355a7475101ff2d8f72842b5c291fcddc7b934d9e6a05c63b71c50443a334.svg) although it is unbounded if ![](images/70/707bb132def3690d81bf1f0c8cf416c1382322ab19d66264cdab1decc4669307.svg) is allowed to approach ![](images/8e/8ef8c99c128deb7895aefe6d1a662021e675d3609017391dd57526f775a30207.svg)\n\n- If ![](images/ba/ba33162d712db6f36bcf09e725d626c399881e98edb06cdc4ddfd17532b06701.svg) then ![](images/88/88318ea5b5d7fde77eace946e939b02b531330ff006ea20c887ea2f654acbdf9.svg) for all ![](images/a9/a90dc11635a0cfd7e959687183800180490f43955de346f70950a5720cd92df4.svg) So, trajectories are *bounded*, and we have an *ellipse*.\n\nLet's see some concrete examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "2a925aab-4f30-57a3-a3a2-6c3e21b5b318" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14482 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7b0e4834-5b7b-5ae6-a7c1-b1def5543b1d" :step/math-academy-id 594290 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167905}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167905 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168010 :tutorial/content "Consider the system of linear differential equations below.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}x(t)-4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{'}(t)=4x(t)+\\frac{1}{2}y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_{1,2} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\pm4\\text{i},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_{1,2} = [\\pm \\text{i}, 1]^T,$ respectively. Given that the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwe are interested in sketching the phase portrait of the system of differential equations given above.\n\nIf we set $c_1 = 1$ and $c_2 = 0$ in the general solution of the system, we obtain the particular solution:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x={e}^{t/2}\\operatorname{cos}(4t) \\\\ y={e}^{t/2}\\operatorname{sin}(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{16}$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $0.78$ | $0$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $0.78$ | $1.22$ |\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](images/4f/4f9da4b66977b98b983477d22abb6e00135ea0e1bfa9f785cd0a62d674a7b782.png)\n\nNotice that $|\\sin(4t)| \\leq 1$ and $|\\cos(4t)| \\leq 1,$ but $e^{t/2} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty.$ Thus, the obtained curve is unbounded. So, we have a spiral that is traversed counterclockwise.\n\nAgain, since $e^{t/2} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ the spiral is traversed toward infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/62/62380812d6fbe4e28531a455814296a4969a0744fb28a94a5e2d7c588ec5cfc4.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "f3d4213d-a0e4-5bda-9e29-8eca4801de63" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14483 :tutorial/title "Systems With Complex Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "89ecb234-3886-5849-970a-abafcfaa1df5" :step/math-academy-id 594291 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167906}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167906 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166941 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166550 :question/id #uuid "8337af7a-73e8-5ae2-9bed-b03f193b2950" :question/math-academy-id "e-21657"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "113b981d-bbf2-588a-be74-028506cdb19d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049672} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050309}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308498} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308501}] :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Phase Portrait of a System"} :step/id #uuid "94fe1e57-9c9a-561e-bfd5-2ae1e6a4369a" :step/math-academy-id 594292 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167907}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167907 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166942 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166551 :question/id #uuid "7b4f569b-ad3a-5a93-a53e-27a186b7700e" :question/math-academy-id "e-21658"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b542bd66-f4f8-5b4e-a3e7-4717827c427a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048535}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308591} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308592} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391460}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Parameters of a System Given Its Phase Portrait"} :step/id #uuid "6468cc1a-a1a6-502b-82f1-16821ba5d8c4" :step/math-academy-id 594295 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167908}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167908 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168025 :tutorial/content "Finally, let’s summarize what we’ve learned so far.\n\nAgain, we are considering a linear system\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the eigenvalues of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_{1,2}=a\\pm bi,\n\\qquad b\\neq 0.\n$$\n\nAs for the classification of the equilibrium at the origin, we get the following:\n\n- The type of the curve is determined by the parameter $a{:}$\n\n- If $a=0,$ we have bounded closed curves (ellipses/circles).\n\n- If $a \\neq 0,$ we have spirals.\n\n- The stability is determined by the sign of $a{:}$\n\n- If $a>0,$ we have an **unstable spiral** (source).\n\n- If $a<0,$ we have a **stable spiral** (sink).\n\n- If $a=0,$ we have a **center**.\n\n**Note:** The center is a *stable* equilibrium but not *asymptotically stable* since trajectories do not converge to it.\n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "c36d256f-edd3-5079-9f72-da6e3a8b4f91" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14575 :tutorial/title "Classification of Equilibria"} :step/id #uuid "6209ec28-abe9-501f-8925-5c8a06aee7df" :step/math-academy-id 594299 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167909}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167909 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166943 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166552 :question/id #uuid "d7b40689-7b7e-546e-9b66-30e6a95f3ee0" :question/math-academy-id "e-21659"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f0b176c-c802-5b3b-85d0-dede602c9bf3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048990}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308593} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308594} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308595} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308596} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308597} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308598}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Systems of Linear Differential Equations With Complex Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "4d18ae8d-2ebf-569f-bebb-2a856d39ef9d" :step/math-academy-id 594300}] :activity/title "Phase Portraits for Linear Systems With Complex Eigenvalues" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186167012}]}
